TY  - JOUR
AU  - Sanchez-Barria, Felipe
TI  - Ideology and the selective legitimation of political violence: Evidence from post-estallido Chile
T2  - INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF PUBLIC OPINION RESEARCH
M3  - Article
AB  - This article examines how citizens evaluate two forms of political violence-violent protest and forceful police repression-and how these evaluations are associated with ideology and political context. Using repeated cross-sectional surveys from Chile between 2019 and 2022, I analyze how support for each varies across ideological groups in distinct post-estallido moments. The results reveal a clear asymmetry. Justification of violent protest remains low throughout and declines across all groups, suggesting it never acquired broad normative legitimacy. Justification of forceful police repression, by contrast, increases substantially over time. Right-wing respondents are consistently the most supportive, but the largest changes occur outside the ideological poles, among centrists and citizens without ideological identification. These two groups are statistically similar to each other throughout the period, yet their willingness to justify repression rises more steeply than the left's or the right's, moving from a position near the left in 2019 toward the right by 2022. Citizens thus do not apply a single normative standard to different forms of political violence. More broadly, the durability of democratic limits on force may depend not only on the ideological poles, but also on less-anchored citizens whose judgments shift more readily as the political context changes.
