TY  - JOUR
AU  - Vargas, Cristobal Rojas
TI  - Translating the Third World: intellectual mediation and political language in the magazine Arauco (Chile, 1959-1966)
T2  - IZQUIERDAS
M3  - Article
AB  - This article examines the role of the Chilean socialist magazine Arauco (1959-1966) in the reception and appropriation of Third World political experiences during the Cold War. Drawing on intellectual history and the study of political transfers, the article analyzes the magazine's role as a space for mediating ideas, practices, and discourses from Africa, Asia, and non-aligned Europe within the context of debates surrounding Chilean socialism. Its pages addressed diverse experiences, including decolonization processes, non-alignment, and Yugoslav socialism. It is argued that, through the selection and circulation of international material, Arauco fostered a dialogue between these external models and the political-strategic issues and debates of Chilean socialism-such as non-alignment, antiimperialism, worker self-management, and socialist democracy. However, each of these experiences took on distinct meanings, depending on the frames of reference and issues under discussion, giving rise to diverse political, ideological, and symbolic uses.
