TY  - JOUR
AU  - Watanabe-Farro, Alejandra
TI  - Discursive geographies of extraction: towards a territorialized application of cultural political economy of extractivism in Chile and Peru
T2  - EXTRACTIVE INDUSTRIES AND SOCIETY
M3  - Article
AB  - Over the past two decades, amid repeated socio-environmental conflicts, mining in Peru and Chile has been rearticulated as environmentally responsible and indispensable to the global Green Energy Transition. This article examines how the resilience of extractivism is built and renewed under conditions that should instead constrain it. It argues that in Chile and Peru, two of the world's leading copper exporters, economic elites, in alliance with the state, systematically translate moments of crisis into revised discursive strategies, governance adjustments, and territorial interventions that secure accumulation across conjunctures. To trace these mechanisms, the article introduces Discursive Geographies of Extraction (DGE), an approach that bridges Cultural Political Economy and Latin American critical geography. DGE centers territory as the site where abstract imaginaries and governance arrangements acquire material durability, harder to reverse than the discourse or the law that authorized them. Methodologically, it combines critical discourse analysis of mining business associations' reports and media coverage with policy analysis, interviews, and event observation. It examines four conflicts: Conga and Pascua Lama during the commodity boom of 2003-2013, and Las Bambas and Los Bronces Integrado during the post-boom period of 2014-2024. Across these sites, crises function as generative junctures through which developmentalist narratives are reformulated into green and sustainability-oriented economic imaginaries, environmental governance is recalibrated, and productive territories are materially produced. The article shows how socio-territorial conflicts, understood as responses to capitalist contradictions in copper extraction, shape the terms of extractivism's reconfiguration, and how extractive industries adapt and shape contemporary green capitalism in Latin America.
