AU  - Orchard, Ximena
AU  - Saldana, Magdalena
AU  - Lazcano-Pena, Daniela
TI  - Emotional reactions to referendum campaigns: how news consumption and political orientations made Chileans feel about the constitutional process
T2  - JOURNAL OF ELECTIONS PUBLIC OPINION AND PARTIES
M3  - Article
M3  - Early Access
AB  - To what extent are emotions shaped by news consumption and political orientations during a referendum campaign? This is the question our study addresses based on the 2022 Chilean Constitutional Referendum, a fraught process initiated with wide citizen support which, nonetheless, ended in citizen rejection at the polls. Using data from a two-wave panel survey, we test complementary models using positive and negative emotions as dependent variables. Our findings confirm that emotions about the referendum are contingent upon certain news consumption regimes. Additionally, we found that individuals on the right and those who rejected the proposal (the "winning" option in the referendum) experienced more negative emotions, suggesting the existence of a group of "sore winners". We discuss how these findings expand previous research about the interaction between news consumption, political orientations and emotions in referendum campaigns.
