TY  - JOUR
AU  - Brandt, Jean Paul
TI  - Photography, aesthetics, and resistance in Chile: political imageries under the lens of Armindo Cardoso
T2  - VISUAL STUDIES
M3  - Article
M3  - Early Access
AB  - This article defines the notion of political imagery of 70s Chile by examining the Portuguese Armindo Cardoso's photographs made in Chile between 1969 and 1973. Through the lens of civil-contract ethics, immigrant duality, and text - image interplay, the study shows how placards, banners and captions work not as ancillary context but as rhetorical agents that mobilise viewers. A curated corpus of eleven images, selected by provenance, display, and reuse history (urban rallies, Mapuche assemblies and agrarian reform scenes) is analysed in relation to Chile's visual field of the period, highlighting how Cardoso's exilic viewpoint negotiates attachment and distance to stage collective agency while respecting ethical exposure. The article also reconstructs the buried archive's material itinerary (1973-2013), clarifying issues of provenance, access and mediation. By situating these visual practices within broader debates on memory politics and post-documentary aesthetics, the article offers visual studies a transferable analytic for understanding how photographic images create civic publics and politicise their own afterlives.
