TY  - JOUR
AU  - Silva-Escobar, Juan Pablo
TI  - Punishing the gaze: the eye as a political image of the 2019 Chilean uprising
T2  - IZQUIERDAS
M3  - Article
AB  - This article examines the visual representations of the eye that emerged during the Chilean social uprising of 2019, in direct relation to the systematic ocular injuries caused by police repression. Drawing on a semi-discursive approach, the study analyzes a heterogeneous corpus of urban interventions, artistic productions, and embodied performative practices as visual dispositifs that articulated political meanings in the public sphere. The article integrates perspectives from political theory and visual studies to conceptualize the eye as an iconic act, an expressive formula (pathosformel), and a floating signifier through which violence, visibility, and resistance were negotiated. It argues that these images functioned simultaneously as acts of denunciation-exposing the punitive rationality of state violence- and as forms of symbolic resistance that reconfigured the distribution of the sensible. Rather than merely documenting repression, ocular imagery actively contributed to the production of collective affects, memories, and processes of political subjectivation. By situating these visual practices within broader debates on punishment, agonism, and aesthetic politics, the article contributes to international discussions on visual culture, protest movements, and state violence, offering a critical perspective on the role of images in contemporary social uprisings.
