AU  - Concha-Diaz, Valeska
AU  - Gallardo-Rodriguez, Rodrigo
TI  - Non-Neutrality of Pedagogy in Early Childhood Education: Ethical, Political, and Epistemological Perspectives of Chilean Female Students
T2  - ANDEAN JOURNAL OF EDUCATION
M3  - Article
AB  - Initial Teacher Training (ITT) in Early Childhood Education is a key aspect of the education system, requiring high-quality specialization focused on the holistic development of early childhood. In this context, promoting a critical understanding of pedagogy and recognizing its non-neutral nature is crucial in the training of future early childhood educators, acknowledging its ethical, political, and epistemological dimensions. However, the profession suffers from a high level of social devaluation, hindering the recognition of its pedagogical character. From this perspective, the objective of this study was to analyze how students pursuing an online degree in Early Childhood Education at a Chilean university interpret and elaborate on the concept of the non-neutrality of pedagogy, considering its ethical, political, and epistemological dimensions, after a reflective training experience. This study employs a qualitative approach with a descriptive-interpretive design. The unit of analysis consisted of 58 students enrolled in an online Early Childhood Education program at a Chilean university. Data were collected through an online questionnaire with open-ended questions, administered after the students participated in an academic forum reflecting on the ethical, political, and epistemological dimensions of pedagogy. The data were analyzed using thematic analysis with axial coding. The findings of this study show that epistemological, ethical, and political dimensions constitute the main reference points from which the students perceive and interpret that pedagogy cannot be neutral, since it is mediated by values, beliefs, and ideological positions that impact pedagogical decisions. The study concludes that recognizing the non-neutrality of pedagogy, acknowledging that teachers' decisions are mediated by values, beliefs, and positions that guide educational practice, is crucial. (c) 2026 L & eacute;niz-Maturana, Rodas-K & uuml;rten, Miranda-Osorio, Hern & aacute;ndez-Y & aacute;& ntilde;ez, Concha-Diaz & Gallardo-Rodriguez. CC BY-NC 4.0
