TY  - JOUR
AU  - Loyola, Manuel Tapia
AU  - Schelchkov, Andrey
TI  - The USSR and Chile: Political and Economic Challenges during the Popular Unity Government
T2  - ISTORIYA-ELEKTRONNYI NAUCHNO-OBRAZOVATELNYI ZHURNAL
M3  - Article
AB  - This work seeks to answer the question: In what way and what was the political support and the intensity of the economic and financial aid of the Soviet Union to the Chilean government of Salvador Allende? To do this, it takes into account the secondary literature that has been produced in this regard, but, in particular, it includes the consultation that we have been able to make to numerous published documents and that until now were unpublished from official sources of the USSR and the Soviet Communist Party itself. Our hypothesis is that the collaboration provided by Moscow, beyond its promises, was generally limited and of little relevance and was never able to respond to the most pressing needs and emergencies that the Allende government went through, committed to a reform program radicals with socialist connotations.From the study, it seems obvious not so much the reluctance and disbelief of the USSR in the success of the "Chilean path to socialism" as the inability of both sides to build a rational system of economic exchange and assistance of the Chilean government due to internal contradictions and the lack of a clear line of development of its own economy, and bureaucracy, inefficiency, and empty talk of the Soviet side, which was not ready for close cooperation with Chile.
