TY  - JOUR
AU  - Padilla-Arenas, Fabian
TI  - Visual component, ideology and emotions in fact-checked disinformation during Chile's 2022 constitutional process
T2  - COMUNICACION Y MEDIOS
M3  - Article
AB  - This article examines the relationship between disinformation format -items with or without a visual component- and two core content features: ideological bias and affective load. Based on a systematic content analysis of 756 items verified during 2022 by the five main Chilean fact-checking platforms active during the constitutional process, three hypotheses are tested, derived from an integrative model linking dual information processing, affective response and the mobilization of political identification (Brader, 2006; Graber, 1990; Powell et al., 2015). Results show that, contrary to expectations, items with a visual component do not exhibit more ideological bias than purely textual ones, but they do display manifest affective load in a significantly higher proportion. Ideological bias, in both formats, favors right-leaning positions. Exploratory analysis of affective valence reveals an unexpected pattern: positive appeals more than double negative ones (72.6% vs 27.4% within the emotional subset), challenging the dominant view in disinformation research.
