TY  - JOUR
AU  - Blanco, Mercedes
TI  - Disillusionment as a diagnosis of a political-military failure: Disillusionment and reparation of the war of the Kingdom of Chile by Alonso Gonzalez de Najera
T2  - E-SPANIA-REVUE ELECTRONIQUE D ETUDES HISPANIQUES MEDIEVALES
M3  - Article
AB  - Disenchantment and Repair of the War of the Kingdom of Chile, written by a veteran from Cuenca who rose to the rank of Field Master thanks to his military merits, is a memorial or arbitration that proposes to King Philip III, through the mediation of the Count of Lemos, a strategy to win the war in Chile. At the same time, it is a literary work intended to communicate to a wide audience all that this war offers that is marvelous and pathetic. Contrary to what has been said, it is not a hybrid work of an indefinable genre, but rather a piece of persuasive eloquence, carefully constructed and elegantly written. Its narrative fragments function in the manner of the narrationes that are part, according to the rhetoricians, of argumentative discourse, while taking on an epic tone. In invoking disenchantment, Gonzalez de Najera refers, like the moralists of the Stoic sign, to the convenience of destroying an illusion. However, for him, and this is where the diametric difference lies, freeing oneself from this illusion does not consist of giving up the false goods of this world, but in abandoning a mistaken strategy to achieve a purely worldly end: the domination of Chile by the Spanish.
