TY  - JOUR
AU  - Torrent, Juan Carlos Rodriguez
AU  - Callegari, Rodrigo Vargas
TI  - Political anthropology of south-south migration: residence and citizenship in neoliberal Chile at the beginning of the XXI century
T2  - IZQUIERDAS
M3  - Article
AB  - The South-South migration process defines the continent's own genetics associated with deep systemic inequality, as well as regional and global policies of inclusion and exclusion typical of the neoliberal State. The context corresponds to a historical development scheme of concentration of economic and political opportunities at the national level, in which imbalances are accentuated that modify the relationships between groups, economic sectors and regions. This places migrant subjects in tense dynamics in the field of culture, democracy, interrelationships, recognition and governability, in the face of the idea of the nation and institutional capacities. With a structural approach and with an emphasis on political anthropology in which power relations are expressed in the sphere of everyday life, this article critically reflects on the difficulties of migratory projects and construction of citizenship in current Chile, within a transversal problematization that goes beyond what is strictly migratory. It is methodologically supported by a set of bibliographic references that go beyond what is strictly migratory and interviews carried out in recent years, collecting expressions of subjectivity that confirm the difficulties of being a migrant within the framework of neoliberal democracy.
