TY  - JOUR
AU  - Camacho Padilla, Fernando
TI  - Political relations between Sweden and Chile during the government of the Christian Democratic Party (1964-1970)
T2  - CANADIAN JOURNAL AMERICAN AND CARIBBEAN STUDIES
M3  - Article
AB  - During the second half of the 1960s, bilateral relations between Chile and Sweden deepened significantly. On the one hand, the Christian Democrat President Eduardo Frei Montalva had a special interest in strengthening his foreign relations in order to receive international support for his reformist policy. On the other hand, Latin America began to be an area of interest in Sweden during the latest years of the presidency of Tage Erlander, especially for the left sectors that felt identified with the revolutionary projects that began to emerge in the region. In addition, there were other factors, such as the arrival of a new generation of Swedish political leaders in the leadership of the Social Democratic Party with a greater internationalist commitment, such as Olof Palme. Similarly, Sweden had just become a world reference for development, equality and prosperity, which created interest in Frei's government. This article presents and analyzes the main events of the political relations between Chile and Sweden from 1964 to 1970. In order to reach this goal, the diplomatic archives of both countries have been thoroughly consulted.
