TY  - JOUR
AU  - LEE,, HO SHIN
AU  - Jin-Yong, Kim，
TI  - Political Analysis of the Characteristics of Tertiary Education and Public Support Process in Korea and Chile
TI  - 한국과 칠레의 고등교육과 공공 지원의 정치적 분석
T2  - The Journal of Asian Studies
T2  - 아시아연구
M3  - research-article
AB  - Both Korea and Chile have the highest tertiary enrollment rates among OECD countries. Both countries also have a high proportion of private universities and the highest tuition rate to GDP. Since the 2000s, the two countries have risen in higher education enrollment rates, but public tuition support has also emerged due to the burden of continuous tuition increases. Korea and Chile have gone in different directions with this issue.In the early 2010s, in Korea, the issue seemed to be accepted into politics as an election pledge during the presidential process. On the other hand, Chile was promised public support as a result of massive student protests. Korea and Chile have a similar higher education situation, but public support promises have taken different routes: election pledges and demonstrations. Besides this, there is a difference in the process of continuing to promote public support. The other directions that Korea and Chile take in a state of universalization of tertiary education are explained by the relatively more and less number of higher educational institutions as positional goods, the union support gap, and electoral systems. This means that there can be different directions depending on whether a single interest is represented or interests are divided.
